# a scheduled module as one wasm module in mink's container, a relocatable
# object or a final module as the build asks
#
# a final module is written with every index and address its own: the
# functions another module defines are imported first, from the module the
# environment names for its imports, then every function the module
# defines, each of the type its placement gives. a function whose address is
# taken has a slot in the one table, from 1 so no slot is the null address,
# filled by an active element segment. the module's globals are laid out in
# linear memory from the host's image base, one active data segment their
# initial values, and the stack grows down from that base, its pointer the
# first global, as mink's link lays memory out stack first. the memory is
# exported as `memory`, and so is every function the module defines that
# other images see, by its name. masc's stream holds the types, the index
# spaces and the labels, and its container states the sections.
#
# a relocatable object is a valid module binary with a "linking" section, as
# tool-conventions' Linking.md states: its functions another module defines
# imported as a final module's are, and what the link defines imported by the
# name mink's link gives it (mink.format.wasm.made), its linear memory as
# env.__linear_memory, its stack pointer as env.__stack_pointer, and where a
# body calls through the table, the table as env.__indirect_function_table.
# without Linking.md's call-indirect-overlong feature a call_indirect's table
# is a single byte no relocation reaches, so the table is named by its import
# alone. every global the module defines is a data segment of its
# own, named by its section or by the kind of section mink names its
# placement, then a dot and its name, laid out from 0 so each has an address
# in the object's own layout, and a global another module defines is an
# undefined data symbol. the symbol table names every function, every global
# and the stack pointer in the order mirl.target.wasm.relocation gives, each
# bound and shown by its linkage, and the segment info names, aligns and
# flags each segment. every symbolic operand of a body and every address in
# an initial value is a relocation of reloc.CODE or reloc.DATA at its offset
# in its section's payload, so each count and size is stated at its shortest
# width rather than left to the writer. the object exports nothing and has no
# table or element segment of its own, as the link lays them out.

